.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.\" Hand-written: the knobs are many and their comments long, so this page
.\" says where each group is and what it does, and points at
.\" config.toml.example (installed in /usr/share/doc/velarx), which the
.\" project keeps complete and which a test of src/ holds in step with this
.\" page's sections.
.TH VELARX.CONF 5 "" "velarx" "Velarx"
.SH NAME
velarx.conf \- the configuration file of velarx
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/velarx/config.toml
.br
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /velarx/config.toml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The configuration of the engine, the command line, the monitor and the
frontends
.RB ( velarx\-api (8)),
in the TOML format.  The root of the host reads
.BR /etc/velarx/config.toml ;
everybody else
.IB $XDG_CONFIG_HOME /velarx/config.toml
.RI ( ~/.config/velarx/config.toml ).
The file is optional: every knob has a default.  The engine of a user is
independent of the one of the root (another store, another state
directory, another configuration), and a setting of the command line
.RB ( \-\-root ", " \-\-runtime ", " \-\-events\-backend ", ...)"
takes the place of the knob it matches.
.PP
Every knob, its default and a comment that says what it does are in
.IR config.toml.example ,
installed in
.BR /usr/share/doc/velarx/config.toml.example ,
which the project keeps complete.  What follows is what each section is
for.
.SH SECTIONS
.TP
.B server
The service of the command line: its sockets and directories
.RB ( socket ", " state_dir ", " root_dir ),
the shutdown timeout, the OCI runtime and its options
.RB ( runtime ", " runtime_args ,
the table
.BR [runtimes] ),
how the cgroups of the containers are made
.RB ( cgroup_manager ),
the store service and where the events are kept.
.TP
.B engine
The engine service
.RB ( "velarx varlink" ):
its socket and how long it stays with no client connected
.RB ( idle_exit ).
.TP
.B services
The limits of each varlink service: the engine, the store, the fetchers
and the services of external sources
.RB ( max_connections ", " max_per_client ", " first_call_secs );
the fetcher the engine shares with the monitors of its
containers has its own
.RB ( "[pull] fetcher_connections" ).
.TP
.B api
.BR velarx\-api (8):
where it listens, the token of its TCP listeners and whether it starts the
engine.
.TP
.B storage
The store: its root, the storage driver
.RB ( driver :
.B composefs
is the default, the EROFS image of each layer;
.B vfs
is a full copy of the files of the image for
each container, made when it is created (the cost of a container is linear in the size of its
image, reflinks make it cheap where the file system has them; it needs no
mount, and takes no
.BR \-\-lazy ", " "\-\-mount type=image" " nor " "\-\-storage\-opt size" ;
it is never chosen for you;
.B varlink
passes the storage to a program of its own that serves
.B org.velarx.Storage
on the unix socket of the table
.BR [storage.varlink] :
.B socket
(an absolute path, required),
.B timeout_secs
(how long a call may be silent, 60 by default: a long call is answered with progress
at least every half of it, the others whole within it) and
.B name
(the name the service must give itself); the engine connects when it starts,
and a service that is not running or speaks another version is an error that
says so, as is a setting that the service does not advertise);
a store keeps the driver it was made with, and
opening it with another is an error), the fs\-verity of its files, which
.B fsverity_require
makes necessary for every file, and which a driver that checks it only when a
layer is pulled (vfs) accepts, for
.BR fsverity_require ", " "composefs_digests = \(dqrequire\(dq" " and " "\-\-security\-opt verity=enforce" ,
only with
.B fsverity_best_effort = true
(the check is at pull and nothing is promised while a container runs; without
it those settings are an error with vfs; a process that may not give files
the owners of the image leaves them its own with
.BR vfs_ignore_chown_errors ),
the EROFS images of the
layers
.RB ( composefs ),
the digests of those images that a manifest states (the values of other
writers in the list are ignored), which every start of a container checks,
strictly with
.B require
.RB ( composefs_digests ),
whether a start holds the files of an image to the chain of trust from the
digest it was made from, or a signature the policy accepts, where the file
system has fs\-verity and every layer was pulled with it
.RB ( verity_chain ,
.BR auto ,
.B off
or
.BR require ),
the read\-only stores of other instances
.RB ( additional_image_stores ),
the garbage collection and how a
pull makes its files durable.
.TP
.B pull
The pulls: what is shared with the peers, the layers fetched at the same
time, the memory and the cgroups of the workers, the limits that stop a
runaway layer or archive
.RB ( max_layer_gib ", " max_inflate_ratio ", " min_speed_kib ),
the signature policy, the lazily pulled images and the background process that
completes their pull
.RB ( lazy_complete_fetches ", " lazy_completers ", " lazy_retry_max_secs ),
the fetcher
.RB ( fetcher_connections ),
and the sources of the content
.RB ( sources ,
.BR [[sources.external]] ).
.TP
.B push
The pushes: the layers regenerated at the same time and the size of the
pieces of an upload
.RB ( parallel_layers ", " chunk_mib ),
the compression of the layers that are regenerated
.RB ( compression ", " compression_level ),
whether the pushers are told where a blob may be linked from
.RB ( mount ),
whether they are asked what the destination has before a layer is
regenerated
.RB ( skip_existing ),
the helper pushers of every push, in order
.RB ( sinks ,
names of
.BR [[sinks.external]] ;
the destination's own pusher always follows them and is not named),
and the process of each scheme that is none of velarx's own
.RB ( [[transports]] :
.BR scheme ", " socket " or " command ", " idle_timeout_secs " and " credentials ;
a top level table for both directions: it serves
.B org.velarx.Fetcher
and/or
.B org.velarx.Pusher
on its socket and announces the word
.B origin
in the
.B GetInfo
of each interface it is the origin for).
A pusher that says nothing for
.B idle_timeout_secs
in a call ends the call (a long
.B Put
must send
.B Progress
events; the wait for the engine's own connection does not count); the push fails naming it, unless it is a helper of
.BR "[push] sinks" ,
which is left out.
A transport gets the credentials of
.I SCHEME://AUTHORITY
only, and only with
.BR "credentials = true" .
The process serving the
.B socket
of a transport must run as the user of the engine or as root; the engine
checks it
.RB ( SO_PEERCRED )
on every connection and refuses another.
.TP
.B sinks
The helper pushers
.RB ( [[sinks.external]] ):
separate processes that speak
.IR org.velarx.Pusher ,
asked before the destination's own pusher
.RB ( name ", " socket " or " command ", " idle_timeout_secs ", " credentials ).
The process serving
.B socket
must run as the user of the engine or as root; the engine checks it
.RB ( SO_PEERCRED )
on every connection and refuses another.
Instead of
.B socket
an entry (a source, a sink or a
.BR [[transports]] )
may have a
.BR command :
a list, the absolute path of a program and its arguments (no shell), exactly
one of the two.
The engine starts it for each use, with the connection to it as its
descriptor 3, nothing added to its arguments, an empty environment and the
user of the engine; the program leaves at the end of the connection, and the
engine kills it when it is silent for
.BR idle_timeout_secs .
It is for a program that keeps nothing between uses.
.TP
.B build
The builds: what an extraction may write, the builds that run or wait at
the same time, the scratch space, the entitlements a build may ask for, and
the algorithm of the digests of the images that are made
.RB ( digest ,
.B sha256
by default, or
.BR sha512 ;
.B \-\-digest
overrides it, it never applies to a pull, and
.B commit
follows the image of its container).
.TP
.B containers
The containers: the log driver and its files
.RB ( log_driver ", " log_max_size ,
.BR log_max_files ", " log_path_allowlist ),
the signature a container's image must have
.RB ( image_signature ;
under
.B require
an image without a name, no tag and never pulled, cannot be judged and is
refused, as under
.BR "\-\-security\-opt signature=check" ),
the infra containers of pods, the CDI devices, the output of a container
and its clients, the cgroups and the network mode of a container that asks
for neither, the
.I /etc/hosts
of the containers, the keys that detach, and the pids limit of a container
that gives none
.RB ( pids_limit ,
2048; 0 or less: none).
.TP
.B userns
The automatic user namespaces of the root: the ranges of the ids, the user
that delegates them and the sizes of the namespaces.
.TP
.B network
The networks: the bridge
.RB ( [network.bridge] ),
the CNI plugins
.RB ( [network.cni] ),
the subnets of the new networks
.RB ( [[network.subnet_pools]] )
and the providers
.RB ( [[network.providers]] ,
separate processes that speak
.IR org.velarx.Network ).
.TP
.B sources
The external sources of content
.RB ( [[sources.external]] ):
separate processes that speak
.IR org.velarx.Fetcher ,
in the place of or before the registry.
The process serving
.B socket
must run as the user of the engine or as root; the engine checks it
.RB ( SO_PEERCRED )
on every connection and refuses another.
The table
.B settings
of a source (sub-tables allowed) is for the program that serves it, which
reads this file itself; the engine keeps it as it is and never reads it.
.BR velarx\-p2p (8)
reads the settings of the source named
.B p2p
(or the name given with
.BR \-\-source );
its keys are in
.BR config.toml.example .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velarx (1),
.BR velarx\-api (8)
